1
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:08,480
Okay, now that you've understood some of the basics of autoimmunity, we're going to talk about a few specific disorders.

2
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:11,720
There are many disorders that are thought to be due to autoimmunity.

3
00:00:11,860 --> 00:00:14,340
We'll cover some of them as we go through this course.

4
00:00:14,660 --> 00:00:17,740
In this section, we're going to cover some of the prototypic disorders.

5
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:22,900
And the most classic disorder that's associated with autoimmunity is this first disorder,

6
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:28,640
which is called systemic lupus erythematosus, or also often known as simply lupus.

7
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:30,800
Now, this is a chronic disorder.

8
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:34,480
Once patients develop it, they essentially have it for the rest of their life.

9
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:39,720
It's a systemic disorder, which basically means that it involves multiple organ systems.

10
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:41,120
And, of course, it's autoimmune.

11
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:44,720
This disorder will be associated with flares and remissions.

12
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:47,640
So the patients will get a flare in which the symptoms will become worse.

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:49,160
And then that will remit.

14
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:51,200
And then over time, they'll develop another flare.

15
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:52,760
And then that will remit.

16
00:00:52,860 --> 00:00:54,700
And then over time, they develop another flare.

17
00:00:54,780 --> 00:00:58,060
And that's a common state of occurrence for patients with lupus.

18
00:00:58,060 --> 00:01:03,820
It classically arises in a middle-aged female, as is relatively common with autoimmune disorders,

19
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:06,060
that demographic of middle-aged females.

20
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:10,280
And it is more common in African-Americans and Hispanics.

21
00:01:10,780 --> 00:01:16,460
Now, I should note that it can occur in children, and it can also occur in older adults.

22
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:19,440
However, the female bias is less dramatic.

23
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:24,060
So, for example, if you look at individuals that are middle-aged who develop lupus,

24
00:01:24,340 --> 00:01:29,080
for every ten females that develop lupus, one male will develop lupus.

25
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:34,820
Whereas, if you look at children and older adults, for every two females that develop lupus,

26
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:37,820
one male will develop lupus.

27
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:41,420
And so the female bias is less dramatic in children and adults.

28
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:46,840
That kind of makes sense, based on one of the principles that we talked about previously.

29
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:52,740
And that is that, remember, the notion of estrogen being somehow involved in autoimmunity.

30
00:01:52,900 --> 00:02:00,680
And that estrogen, one thing that's been shown is that estrogen reduces the apoptosis of self-reactive B cells.

31
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:05,940
And so, for example, we know that middle-aged females would have a lot of estrogen floating around.

32
00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:08,540
And so that would increase the risk for females.

33
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:12,320
Whereas, when you look at children and you look at older adults,

34
00:02:12,420 --> 00:02:16,140
children would have less of an estrogen differential between males and females.

35
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:21,420
And older adults, as well, would have a less differential of estrogen between males and females.

36
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:27,280
And so perhaps that helps to explain why there's less of a female bias in that particular age group.

37
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:29,620
Now, if I were to summarize this disorder,

38
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:36,280
I would say that this is a disorder in which antigen-antibody complexes deposit in multiple tissues.

39
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:41,720
This then results in the activation of complement, which then damages those tissues.

40
00:02:42,380 --> 00:02:46,000
So essentially, because this involves antigen-antibody complexes,

41
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:50,480
this is a type 3 hypersensitivity reaction.

42
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:53,460
In addition, because complement gets activated,

43
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:57,360
we end up using the complement factors that are present within our blood.

44
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:00,180
And so the patients will have a decreased CH50.

45
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:04,140
CH50 is a test which looks at the function of the patient's complement.

46
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:07,980
And it particularly looks at C1 all the way through C9

47
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:11,880
and tests the ability of the patient's complement to be able to life-spread blood cells.

48
00:03:12,360 --> 00:03:14,440
And so if there's a decrease in the CH50,

49
00:03:14,540 --> 00:03:16,840
it indicates that complement components are being used.

50
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:21,920
The patient's C3 and C4 levels will also be decreased.

51
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:26,180
And that's because they're using complement components as tissues are getting damaged.

52
00:03:26,180 --> 00:03:31,640
The last point that I'll make is that the antibodies that are generated in this particular disorder

53
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:34,820
are directed against host nuclear material.

54
00:03:35,240 --> 00:03:38,260
So again, we have antibodies against host nuclear material,

55
00:03:38,460 --> 00:03:41,720
which eventually result in antibody-antigen complexes,

56
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:45,160
which will then deposit in those tissues, activate complement,

57
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:47,460
and eventually cause tissue damage.

58
00:03:48,180 --> 00:03:53,100
Now let's go back to the whiteboard to understand a little bit about the pathogenesis of this disorder.

59
00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:59,320
So what's currently thought is that, for example, if this is the epidermis of the skin,

60
00:03:59,560 --> 00:04:03,420
and what I'm drawing here, each square represents a keratinocyte.

61
00:04:04,660 --> 00:04:09,240
Imagine if UV light comes from the outside and hits the keratinocytes of the skin.

62
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:10,860
It'll damage some DNA.

63
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:14,920
And as it damages DNA, that can result in apoptosis of some of the cells.

64
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:17,440
So let's assume that a cell undergoes apoptosis.

65
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:22,920
You already know that when cells undergo apoptosis, the cytoplasm shrinks, the nucleus also shrinks,

66
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:26,680
and eventually little apoptotic bodies are released,

67
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:30,440
and this apoptotic debris is eventually taken up by macrophages.

68
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:38,380
Now let's assume that there is some decreased clearance or poor clearance of this apoptotic debris.

69
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:42,280
Now as apoptotic debris hangs around for a little bit longer,

70
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:48,700
for example, a self-reactive B lymphocyte may get exposed to that apoptotic debris,

71
00:04:49,140 --> 00:04:50,600
hence it would see nuclear material.

72
00:04:50,740 --> 00:04:58,360
And when it sees nuclear material, it would then begin to produce antibody versus nuclear antigens.

73
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:01,580
So as antibodies produced against nuclear antigens,

74
00:05:01,580 --> 00:05:08,820
the next time UVV light hits the keratinocyte and causes apoptosis again,

75
00:05:09,080 --> 00:05:13,380
which then results in release of nuclear material,

76
00:05:14,040 --> 00:05:16,760
the antibody could bind this nuclear material,

77
00:05:17,060 --> 00:05:21,500
and now you're going to have antigen-antibody complexes at low levels.

78
00:05:22,160 --> 00:05:27,160
Now these antigen-antibody complexes can be taken up by dendritic cells.

79
00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:29,200
So let's say this is a dendritic cell.

80
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:31,660
And once they're taken up by dendritic cells,

81
00:05:32,020 --> 00:05:33,760
the antigen, which is nuclear material,

82
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:38,700
can actually activate the toll-like receptors present within dendritic cells.

83
00:05:39,180 --> 00:05:43,840
These toll-like receptors, once activated, will then amplify the immune response.

84
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:48,080
And when they amplify the immune response, they'll further activate these B cells.

85
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:54,120
As they further activate the B cells, there'll be additional antibody produced.

86
00:05:54,120 --> 00:06:02,880
And as additional antibodies produced, now we will have high levels of antigen-antibody complexes forming

87
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:07,840
the next time that there is apoptosis of a keratinocyte, for example.

88
00:06:08,120 --> 00:06:12,580
These high levels of antigen-antibody complexes can enter into the blood, for example,

89
00:06:12,700 --> 00:06:14,240
and then deposit in multiple tissues.

90
00:06:14,620 --> 00:06:19,720
And as they deposit in multiple tissues, they can result in the activation of complement,

91
00:06:19,720 --> 00:06:22,180
which can then damage tissues.

92
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:28,720
And so this is the current sort of paradigm of how patients might develop lupus.

93
00:06:29,540 --> 00:06:33,220
One of the observations that helps to support this theory of pathogenesis

94
00:06:33,220 --> 00:06:39,780
is the fact that patients with early complement deficiency have an increased risk of developing lupus.

95
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:45,200
In order to understand this, remember that the disorder is a problem of the formation of antibodies

96
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:47,520
against host nuclear material.

97
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:49,400
So here's your host nuclear material.

98
00:06:49,980 --> 00:06:56,840
And when you have these antigen-antibody complexes, complement is essential for clearing antigen-antibody complexes.

99
00:06:57,420 --> 00:07:02,480
For example, when you have antibody bound to antigen, in this particular case, this would be IgG,

100
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:12,720
then C1 will attach, and when C1 attaches, eventually C4 will come by, and C4 will be cleaved.

101
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:16,740
And when C4 is cleaved, eventually C2 will attach, and C2 will be cleaved.

102
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:24,660
And when you have the C1-Q-C2-C4 complex, that's also a C3 convertase.

103
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:26,840
So that would form a C3 convertase.

104
00:07:26,840 --> 00:07:35,660
And the C3 convertase, of course, would take C3, and would cleave it to C3A and C3B.

105
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:39,300
And C3B, remember, is an important opsonin.

106
00:07:39,420 --> 00:07:46,580
So as C3B begins to attach to this complex, it would act as an opsonin,

107
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:51,080
which would then allow for removal of this antigen-antibody complex.

108
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:59,140
For example, macrophages have the ability to recognize C3B and will use it as a means to take up this antigen-antibody complex.

109
00:07:59,580 --> 00:08:06,120
Similarly, if the antigen-antibody complex is in the blood, erythrocytes have a receptor on them, which is called CR1.

110
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:09,780
And CR1 has the ability to bind C3B.

111
00:08:10,300 --> 00:08:16,300
Now, when this binds to the antigen-antibody complex, it will take the antigen-antibody complex to the spleen,

112
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:20,560
and in the spleen, the macrophages will then eat and remove the antigen-antibody complex.

113
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:25,480
So essentially, what we need to recognize is that complement is necessary.

114
00:08:25,720 --> 00:08:37,080
In particular, the early complement components of the classical pathway are necessary to remove and clear antigen-antibody complexes.

115
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:42,340
And so it wouldn't be too hard to imagine that if we had a deficiency of these early complement proteins,

116
00:08:42,560 --> 00:08:45,560
that we would have an increased risk of developing lupus.

117
00:08:45,560 --> 00:08:51,160
And in fact, patients with deficiency of early complement proteins, half of them will develop lupus.

118
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:58,160
And the most common complement deficiency to arise among these three would be a deficiency of C2.

119
00:08:58,560 --> 00:09:05,400
And so these are some things that you should be aware of concerning early complement deficiency and its association with lupus.

120
00:09:06,900 --> 00:09:10,440
Clinically, patients with lupus will have a whole bunch of findings.

121
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:16,400
And I know that sometimes students have difficulty remembering these, but if you put them together, it's not too difficult.

122
00:09:16,560 --> 00:09:19,860
So first, let's start with a few of the nonspecific findings.

123
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:24,260
So the patients are going to have fever, they're going to have weight loss, and they're going to have fatigue.

124
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:31,560
And I don't think that's too difficult to remember, because anybody who's got activation of the immune system is going to have fever, weight loss, and fatigue.

125
00:09:31,560 --> 00:09:38,200
Similarly, they're going to have lymphadenopathy, where there will be expansion of the lymph nodes, and the lymph nodes will become big.

126
00:09:38,440 --> 00:09:42,000
And the reason the lymph nodes become big is, again, you've got activation of the immune system.

127
00:09:42,320 --> 00:09:44,140
So all of these findings are nonspecific.

128
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:52,000
Although they're very common in patients with lupus, they don't help us to make a firm diagnosis of lupus, because they can be seen in many other disorders.

129
00:09:52,000 --> 00:10:08,820
In addition, patients with lupus will also have something called Raynaud phenomena, and that's a circumstance where patients get arteriovasal spasm so that you can see this change in color, usually in their fingertips or on the tip of their nose, for example, where it changes from white to blue to red.

130
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:13,100
If you want to understand Raynaud, just press on your fingertip, and you'll notice that it turns white.

131
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:16,200
If you were to cut the blood supply for long enough, it would turn blue.

132
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:22,200
And when you would release the vasoconstriction and the blood would come back in, it would again turn red.

133
00:10:22,300 --> 00:10:26,600
And simply press down on your finger, it turns white, release your finger, and you'll see that it turns red.

134
00:10:26,940 --> 00:10:29,280
So that's the exact sequence that occurs in Raynaud's.

135
00:10:29,340 --> 00:10:36,740
There's a vasospasm of the artery, which kind of cuts the blood supply for a few minutes, and that then turns the finger white.

136
00:10:36,980 --> 00:10:39,520
Over time, as there's ischemia, it turns blue.

137
00:10:39,520 --> 00:10:45,460
And then eventually, when the vasospasm releases, the red color comes back into the organ.

138
00:10:45,460 --> 00:10:47,640
In particular, the fingertips or the nose.

139
00:10:47,800 --> 00:11:00,220
And this is often associated with cold or with stress, meaning patients will get this white to blue to red change when there is cold temperature or when they're undergoing stress.

140
00:11:00,780 --> 00:11:02,560
Raynaud phenomena is very nonspecific.

141
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,460
It can be seen in patients who have no autoimmune disorders.

142
00:11:05,460 --> 00:11:11,020
In fact, when patients have Raynaud's, they often don't have any other autoimmune disorder.

143
00:11:11,120 --> 00:11:13,840
Or it can be seen in association with multiple autoimmune disorders.

144
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:16,080
So, again, I put that in the nonspecific list.

145
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:24,280
What would be a little more specific for lupus is a malar butterfly rash or a discoid rash, especially on exposure to sunlight.

146
00:11:25,100 --> 00:11:28,800
The malar butterfly rash is a rash that's seen over the cheekbones.

147
00:11:29,380 --> 00:11:32,420
And here is an image of such a malar butterfly rash.

148
00:11:32,460 --> 00:11:36,000
So it's called a butterfly rash because of this pattern here.

149
00:11:36,060 --> 00:11:38,040
And, of course, this is seen over the cheekbones.

150
00:11:38,040 --> 00:11:44,080
And so that's a classic rash that can occur in patients, especially when they are exposed to sunlight.

151
00:11:44,320 --> 00:11:47,120
So that's a feature of lupus that you should be aware of.

152
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:49,620
Now, again, one feature does not make lupus.

153
00:11:49,700 --> 00:11:52,420
And malar butterfly rash can be seen in other disorders as well.

154
00:11:52,680 --> 00:11:56,140
However, it is an important diagnostic criteria for this particular disorder.

155
00:11:56,740 --> 00:11:59,680
Another type of rash that can be seen is something called the discoid rash.

156
00:11:59,680 --> 00:12:05,580
This is a rash which is circular and erythematous with scaling that can scar.

157
00:12:06,140 --> 00:12:08,280
In fact, here's a picture of a discoid rash.

158
00:12:08,360 --> 00:12:11,240
And you can see this kind of looks like a circular or disc-like rash.

159
00:12:11,560 --> 00:12:13,180
You can see that it is erythematous.

160
00:12:13,240 --> 00:12:15,720
You can see that there is scaling on this rash.

161
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:17,240
And over time, this would scar.

162
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,160
It's this type of rash that actually led to the name lupus.

163
00:12:21,320 --> 00:12:22,760
Lupus literally means wolf.

164
00:12:22,760 --> 00:12:28,560
And it was thought that this disorder arose when a person was bit by a wolf in the middle of the night.

165
00:12:28,900 --> 00:12:30,780
And so they would get these lesions that would scar.

166
00:12:30,960 --> 00:12:35,220
And so people would call this lupus because they thought that this was due to a wolf bite.

167
00:12:35,620 --> 00:12:38,520
And so it is important to be aware of this classic lesion.

168
00:12:39,320 --> 00:12:42,020
And this is also one of the diagnostic criteria as well.

169
00:12:42,460 --> 00:12:46,420
So a malar rash or a discoid rash, especially upon exposure to sunlight.

170
00:12:46,940 --> 00:12:50,620
Now, there are 11 official criteria for lupus.

171
00:12:50,620 --> 00:12:55,280
And of those 11, number 1 is a malar rash, number 2 is a discoid rash,

172
00:12:55,340 --> 00:12:57,680
and number 3 is a rash upon exposure to sunlight.

173
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,800
So 1, 2, and 3 are taken care of right there.

174
00:13:01,680 --> 00:13:05,840
Now, I should take a moment to just be clear that although we're going to list 11 criteria,

175
00:13:06,220 --> 00:13:08,820
not all criteria are going to be seen in any particular patient.

176
00:13:08,940 --> 00:13:12,280
In fact, you only need 4 of these criteria, or at least 4 of these criteria,

177
00:13:12,760 --> 00:13:14,700
in order to make a diagnosis of lupus.

178
00:13:15,420 --> 00:13:18,920
Other features that can be seen are oral or nasopharyngeal ulcers.

179
00:13:18,920 --> 00:13:20,480
In fact, this is the fourth criteria.

180
00:13:21,240 --> 00:13:24,820
Arthritis, when patients get arthritis, this usually involves peripheral joints,

181
00:13:24,900 --> 00:13:26,000
usually two or more joints.

182
00:13:26,460 --> 00:13:29,180
The joints will be tender and swelling, but there will be no deformity.

183
00:13:29,620 --> 00:13:31,640
And that is number 5 criteria.

184
00:13:32,120 --> 00:13:36,100
Number 6 criteria is that patients get inflammation of the serosal surfaces.

185
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:40,280
So, for example, they'll get inflammation of the serosa of the lung,

186
00:13:40,380 --> 00:13:41,420
giving them pleuritis.

187
00:13:41,820 --> 00:13:43,640
And that can result in pleuritic pain.

188
00:13:43,760 --> 00:13:45,860
So when patients breathe in, they might feel pain.

189
00:13:45,860 --> 00:13:48,820
Or it could result in a rub that could be heard on auscultation.

190
00:13:49,160 --> 00:13:52,920
Similarly, the patients could get inflammation of the serosal surface of the heart.

191
00:13:53,020 --> 00:13:54,420
That would give them pericarditis.

192
00:13:54,780 --> 00:13:56,420
And that could be detected by EKG.

193
00:13:56,540 --> 00:13:59,560
Or it could be detected by auscultating a rub.

194
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:03,480
Or it could be detected by an effusion in the pericardium.

195
00:14:03,480 --> 00:14:06,220
So, this is a criteria as well.

196
00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:08,880
And serositis is a common finding in lupus.

197
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:16,440
The seventh criteria is CNS problems, in particular psychosis or seizures.

198
00:14:17,100 --> 00:14:19,200
The eighth, so that's the seventh problem.

199
00:14:19,520 --> 00:14:23,320
The eighth criteria is that patients will often have renal damage.

200
00:14:23,720 --> 00:14:27,660
Now, this criteria is particularly important, so I want to spend a moment discussing it.

201
00:14:27,660 --> 00:14:33,220
Renal damage in lupus can be that the patients get something called a nephritic syndrome,

202
00:14:33,820 --> 00:14:37,140
which is going to be characterized by hypertension and hematuria.

203
00:14:37,660 --> 00:14:43,040
They can also get a type of disorder in the kidney, which is called nephrotic syndrome,

204
00:14:43,560 --> 00:14:47,220
which is going to be characterized by high levels of protein leaking out into the urine.

205
00:14:47,740 --> 00:14:51,460
They could get both a combination of nephritic and nephrotic syndrome.

206
00:14:51,940 --> 00:14:54,200
They could have no damage to the kidney.

207
00:14:54,700 --> 00:14:57,340
Damage to the kidney is not always necessary in lupus.

208
00:14:57,340 --> 00:15:01,140
And so, there's a wide spectrum of damage that can occur within the kidney.

209
00:15:01,540 --> 00:15:05,700
But the kidney is very important because it's a common cause of death in patients with lupus,

210
00:15:05,780 --> 00:15:08,720
and so you do need to be aware of what can happen in the kidney.

211
00:15:09,180 --> 00:15:12,000
The highest yield points concerning renal damage are the following.

212
00:15:12,440 --> 00:15:17,580
The first is that the most common and most severe problem that the patients can have in the kidney

213
00:15:17,580 --> 00:15:21,440
is called diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis.

214
00:15:21,880 --> 00:15:23,520
And this is a type of nephritic syndrome.

215
00:15:23,680 --> 00:15:26,180
We'll talk more about nephritic syndrome when you do the renal section,

216
00:15:26,180 --> 00:15:30,880
but you should just bury in the back of your brain that this is a type of nephritic syndrome.

217
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:35,660
Another high yield point is that patients who have proteinuria and nephrotic syndrome,

218
00:15:35,880 --> 00:15:38,560
and again, you'll cover nephrotic syndrome when we do the renal section,

219
00:15:39,040 --> 00:15:42,880
will have a type of damage in the kidney called membranous glomerulonephritis.

220
00:15:43,480 --> 00:15:46,240
So, this is criteria number seven, this being six.

221
00:15:46,240 --> 00:15:51,800
The eighth criteria is that the patients will develop antibodies against red blood cells,

222
00:15:52,020 --> 00:15:55,760
or white blood cells, or platelets, basically some cell within the blood.

223
00:15:56,180 --> 00:15:59,580
And as they develop antibodies against those cells, they'll begin to damage those cells,

224
00:15:59,680 --> 00:16:01,500
and so if it's red cells, they'll get an anemia.

225
00:16:02,020 --> 00:16:04,120
If it's platelets, they'll get a thrombocytopenia.

226
00:16:04,840 --> 00:16:10,560
And if, for example, it's white cells, such as neutrophils or lymphocytes, they'll get a leukopenia.

227
00:16:10,560 --> 00:16:13,240
And so, this is an important finding in lupus patients.

228
00:16:13,780 --> 00:16:15,700
And now, I'd like to make one important point here,

229
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:21,640
and that is this finding is going to be due to a type 2 hypersensitivity reaction.

230
00:16:21,760 --> 00:16:25,260
Remember that, in general, lupus is a type 3 hypersensitivity reaction

231
00:16:25,260 --> 00:16:28,720
where antibody and antigen complexes will deposit in multiple tissues.

232
00:16:29,140 --> 00:16:33,580
However, in patients who have lupus who develop this problem in their blood,

233
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:39,280
generally what happens is they have a direct antibody against the red cell, for example,

234
00:16:39,280 --> 00:16:42,200
and that then results in destruction of the red cell.

235
00:16:42,280 --> 00:16:44,440
And so, that's a type 2 hypersensitivity.

236
00:16:45,400 --> 00:16:50,580
Another feature that can be found in patients with lupus is something called Liebman-Sachs endocarditis.

237
00:16:51,600 --> 00:16:55,180
And the first thing that I'd like to say is that lupus can affect any layer of the heart.

238
00:16:55,300 --> 00:17:00,340
So, for example, lupus can affect the pericardium, and that would give you pericarditis,

239
00:17:00,400 --> 00:17:01,480
and we've already talked about that.

240
00:17:01,820 --> 00:17:04,760
Lupus can affect the myocardium, giving you myocarditis,

241
00:17:04,760 --> 00:17:09,500
and lupus can also affect the endocardium, giving you a Liebman-Sachs endocarditis.

242
00:17:09,960 --> 00:17:15,060
Now, a key finding of Liebman-Sachs endocarditis is that patients get small vegetations

243
00:17:15,060 --> 00:17:17,140
on both sides of the mitral valve.

244
00:17:17,680 --> 00:17:20,620
And, again, this is a historically classic finding,

245
00:17:20,720 --> 00:17:23,620
but it's now rarely seen due to treatment with immunosuppressives.

246
00:17:24,140 --> 00:17:25,980
Now, when patients normally get endocarditis,

247
00:17:26,040 --> 00:17:29,240
let's imagine that this is a picture of the heart, and this is the mitral valve here,

248
00:17:29,240 --> 00:17:34,240
they would get vegetations on the valve, but those vegetations would be on one side of the valve.

249
00:17:34,680 --> 00:17:37,340
In Liebman-Sachs endocarditis, you get vegetations,

250
00:17:37,420 --> 00:17:42,560
and there's small little vegetations that are present on both sides of the valve.

251
00:17:42,920 --> 00:17:48,080
And so this is an important finding that is classic for patients with lupus,

252
00:17:48,140 --> 00:17:52,720
but, again, is not really seen because of the fact that we now treat with immunosuppressives.

253
00:17:52,960 --> 00:17:54,360
But it is something to be aware of.

254
00:17:54,360 --> 00:17:59,480
It is not one of the diagnostic criteria, but, again, something that you should be aware of.

255
00:17:59,980 --> 00:18:04,860
And so on this slide, we've added an additional three criteria, correct?

256
00:18:05,060 --> 00:18:07,140
Seven, eight, and nine.

257
00:18:08,320 --> 00:18:13,860
The tenth criteria is that patients who have lupus will often have an antinuclear antibody.

258
00:18:14,300 --> 00:18:19,120
Now, the antinuclear antibody is an antibody that would be present in the serum of the patient,

259
00:18:19,120 --> 00:18:23,920
and it is good for screening because it is relatively sensitive.

260
00:18:24,460 --> 00:18:26,480
However, it is nonspecific.

261
00:18:26,620 --> 00:18:27,880
And why we say it's nonspecific?

262
00:18:28,020 --> 00:18:33,380
Because, first of all, antinuclear antibodies can be seen in 5% of normal individuals,

263
00:18:33,740 --> 00:18:38,120
and also antinuclear antibodies can be seen in many autoimmune disorders.

264
00:18:38,480 --> 00:18:41,220
So, again, it's sensitive that there might be something going on.

265
00:18:41,260 --> 00:18:42,340
However, it's nonspecific.

266
00:18:42,720 --> 00:18:44,120
So that's criteria number 10.

267
00:18:44,120 --> 00:18:49,840
Criteria number 11 is that you have some antibody beyond the antinuclear antibody.

268
00:18:50,200 --> 00:18:55,920
So, for example, patients can have an anti-double-stranded DNA antibody or an anti-Smith antibody.

269
00:18:56,340 --> 00:19:02,320
And the anti-double-stranded DNA and anti-Smith antibodies are very specific for lupus.

270
00:19:02,720 --> 00:19:04,160
And so this is something to be aware of.

271
00:19:04,360 --> 00:19:08,480
Again, anti-double-stranded DNA and anti-Smith antibodies are specific.

272
00:19:08,700 --> 00:19:10,660
So let's say a patient had a positive ANA.

273
00:19:10,660 --> 00:19:16,640
The next thing we would do, we might test for anti-double-stranded DNA and anti-Smith if we were worried about lupus.

274
00:19:17,080 --> 00:19:22,640
Another point that I would like to make that can show up on examinations is that the anti-double-stranded DNA antibody

275
00:19:22,640 --> 00:19:29,460
is associated with disease activity, and it is also associated with renal nephritis.

276
00:19:29,460 --> 00:19:37,480
And so this antibody can be somewhat prognostic and can also be used to follow disease activity.

277
00:19:37,640 --> 00:19:43,360
For example, if we're worried about a patient developing a relapse, we could test the anti-double-stranded DNA antibody,

278
00:19:43,740 --> 00:19:49,180
and if the titers are high, then we could suggest that the patient is about to relapse and perhaps we might treat.

279
00:19:49,560 --> 00:19:56,340
So anyway, the anti-double-stranded DNA antibody is associated with both prognosis as well as active disease.

280
00:19:56,340 --> 00:20:02,700
Another potential antibody that can be seen in patients with lupus is something called an anti-phospholipid antibody,

281
00:20:02,840 --> 00:20:05,060
and this also forms part of the 11th criteria.

282
00:20:05,540 --> 00:20:08,800
So remember the 10th criteria is the presence of an anti-nuclear antibody.

283
00:20:09,160 --> 00:20:16,860
The 11th criteria is the presence of an anti-double-stranded DNA antibody, anti-Smith antibody, or an anti-phospholipid antibody.

284
00:20:17,420 --> 00:20:25,420
Now anti-phospholipid antibodies are antibodies that bind proteins when those proteins are bound to phospholipids.

285
00:20:25,420 --> 00:20:31,980
What happens is when the protein is bound to phospholipids, the epitopes are revealed that are antigenic,

286
00:20:32,280 --> 00:20:35,280
and that then results in an anti-phospholipid antibody.

287
00:20:35,960 --> 00:20:40,220
There are three anti-phospholipid antibodies that are commonly tested clinically.

288
00:20:40,740 --> 00:20:46,340
The first is anti-cardiolipin antibody, the second is lupus anticoagulant,

289
00:20:46,600 --> 00:20:50,300
and the third is an antibody called anti-beta-2-glycoprotein-1.

290
00:20:50,300 --> 00:20:58,100
The anti-cardiolipid antibody is particularly interesting because it can produce a false positive screening test for syphilis.

291
00:20:58,560 --> 00:21:03,220
Now recall that when we screen for syphilis, we use one of two tests, the VDRL or the RPR,

292
00:21:03,540 --> 00:21:07,700
and these tests are actually looking for the presence of anti-cardiolipid antibodies.

293
00:21:07,860 --> 00:21:12,800
In fact, when patients get infected with syphilis, that results in tissue damage, lipids are released,

294
00:21:12,800 --> 00:21:17,080
and it's common for patients with syphilis to develop anti-cardiolipid antibodies.

295
00:21:17,560 --> 00:21:23,780
And so these two tests actually have cardiolipid as a reagent, and they look for anti-cardiolipid antibodies.

296
00:21:24,200 --> 00:21:28,280
Now let's imagine that a patient has lupus and generates anti-cardiolipid antibodies.

297
00:21:28,660 --> 00:21:30,740
They, of course, could turn this test positive,

298
00:21:31,100 --> 00:21:35,680
and so you would have a false positive reaction in the two screening tests for syphilis.

299
00:21:36,180 --> 00:21:40,360
Now, of course, patients with lupus can also get infected and get syphilis,

300
00:21:40,360 --> 00:21:46,480
and so if you wanted to confirm, you could confirm using the direct or the specific treponemal tests,

301
00:21:46,600 --> 00:21:54,260
and so, for example, one test would be the FTA-ABS test that could be used to confirm any positive screening test for syphilis.

302
00:21:54,960 --> 00:22:02,100
Another important point concerning anti-phospholipid antibodies is that patients can have an anti-phospholipid antibody called lupus anticoagulant.

303
00:22:02,580 --> 00:22:07,260
This anti-phospholipid antibody falsely elevates the patient's PTT test

304
00:22:07,260 --> 00:22:12,220
because it interferes with the phospholipids that are used in the PTT test,

305
00:22:12,540 --> 00:22:15,240
and so these patients will have a falsely elevated PTT.

306
00:22:16,280 --> 00:22:19,540
Now what's interesting about these patients with lupus anticoagulant

307
00:22:19,540 --> 00:22:25,180
is that those patients can also have an associated syndrome called the anti-phospholipid antibody syndrome.

308
00:22:25,900 --> 00:22:31,080
Anti-phospholipid antibody syndrome is a syndrome where the patients have an anti-phospholipid antibody,

309
00:22:31,080 --> 00:22:35,800
usually lupus anticoagulant, plus a hypercoagulable state.

310
00:22:36,380 --> 00:22:41,460
Now when they have this hypercoagulable state, what happens is the patients can get arterial and venous thrombosis.

311
00:22:41,900 --> 00:22:45,740
So, for example, they can get DVTs, they can get hepatic vein thrombosis,

312
00:22:45,900 --> 00:22:49,760
they could get placental thrombosis, which would result in recurrent pregnancy loss,

313
00:22:50,180 --> 00:22:53,180
they could get cerebral thrombosis, which would result in stroke,

314
00:22:53,180 --> 00:22:58,580
and all of these are associated with this anti-phospholipid antibody.

315
00:22:59,160 --> 00:23:03,040
Now, although the anti-phospholipid antibody interferes with the PTT test

316
00:23:03,040 --> 00:23:07,240
and causes the PTT to increase, it's interesting, that's paradoxical,

317
00:23:07,320 --> 00:23:10,500
because actually the patients have a hypercoagulable state,

318
00:23:10,580 --> 00:23:11,900
so that's one thing to be aware of.

319
00:23:12,360 --> 00:23:16,860
Another thing to be aware of is that these patients will then require lifelong anticoagulation.

320
00:23:17,380 --> 00:23:20,920
Now I'd like to say one more thing about the anti-phospholipid antibody syndrome.

321
00:23:20,920 --> 00:23:26,300
It is associated with lupus, however, it more commonly occurs as a primary disorder.

322
00:23:26,820 --> 00:23:31,860
So a patient can have anti-phospholipid antibodies with all the problems that I just stated,

323
00:23:32,220 --> 00:23:36,200
causing the anti-phospholipid antibody syndrome, and doesn't have to have lupus.

324
00:23:36,280 --> 00:23:39,040
In fact, more commonly, they wouldn't have lupus.

325
00:23:39,860 --> 00:23:44,840
The last point that I'd like to make concerning antibodies is that patients that get drug-induced lupus

326
00:23:44,840 --> 00:23:48,480
often have an antibody called an antihistone antibody.

327
00:23:48,880 --> 00:23:49,960
So let's take a step back.

328
00:23:49,960 --> 00:23:53,700
First of all, there are a subset of drugs that can induce lupus,

329
00:23:54,060 --> 00:23:59,640
and the classic drugs to be aware of are hydralazine, procainamide, and isoniazid.

330
00:24:00,060 --> 00:24:02,020
These can induce lupus.

331
00:24:02,440 --> 00:24:08,160
When patients get drug-induced lupus, they will have a positive ANA, anti-nuclear antibody,

332
00:24:08,420 --> 00:24:12,960
and then that ANA will be specific to antihistone antibodies.

333
00:24:13,080 --> 00:24:17,120
So you would test for antihistone antibodies, and the antihistone antibody would be positive.

334
00:24:17,120 --> 00:24:23,080
Now, some other features of drug-induced lupus is that the patients will develop rash,

335
00:24:23,280 --> 00:24:25,700
serositis, and hematologic problems.

336
00:24:26,060 --> 00:24:31,280
However, CNS and renal involvement is rare with drug-induced lupus.

337
00:24:31,800 --> 00:24:34,860
And finally, the last point is that if you remove the drug,

338
00:24:35,100 --> 00:24:38,520
this will usually result in remission of the drug-induced lupus.

339
00:24:38,520 --> 00:24:42,900
Treatment for lupus is that patients should avoid sunlight.

340
00:24:43,120 --> 00:24:48,560
Again, the whole notion that sunlight is damaging DNA, resulting in apoptosis,

341
00:24:48,820 --> 00:24:50,700
resulting in antigen-antibody complexes.

342
00:24:50,900 --> 00:24:52,440
So one thing would be to avoid sunlight.

343
00:24:52,960 --> 00:24:56,000
If the patient undergoes a flare, we could give glucocorticoids.

344
00:24:56,060 --> 00:24:59,360
Of course, what's driving the entire process is the immune system,

345
00:24:59,460 --> 00:25:02,560
and so if you give glucocorticoids, you'd suppress the immune system.

346
00:25:02,560 --> 00:25:06,960
And if the patients have refractory disease or if the patients have severe disease,

347
00:25:07,320 --> 00:25:09,580
other immunosuppressive agents can be used.

348
00:25:10,240 --> 00:25:12,740
Now, with this treatment, the prognosis is fairly good.

349
00:25:12,860 --> 00:25:15,660
There is a greater than 90% five-year survival.

350
00:25:16,020 --> 00:25:20,720
This is important because in the 1950s, the five-year survival was less than 50%.

351
00:25:20,720 --> 00:25:23,400
So treatment has certainly helped the five-year survival.

352
00:25:24,120 --> 00:25:27,980
Renal failure and infection are the most common causes of death.

353
00:25:28,260 --> 00:25:32,140
Renal failure, we've talked about the importance of renal involvement in lupus.

354
00:25:32,520 --> 00:25:35,440
And infection, there's kind of two ways by which patients could get infection.

355
00:25:35,560 --> 00:25:37,840
Number one, we're treating the patients with immunosuppressives,

356
00:25:37,900 --> 00:25:39,200
so that could result in infection.

357
00:25:39,840 --> 00:25:43,300
And number two, remember that patients can generate antibodies to their white cells,

358
00:25:43,380 --> 00:25:46,880
and so if they damage the white cells, that would increase the risk of infection as well.

359
00:25:47,300 --> 00:25:49,840
So renal failure and infection are the most common causes of death.

360
00:25:50,080 --> 00:25:52,060
Again, I'll remind you of two high-yield points here.

361
00:25:52,400 --> 00:25:56,100
Patients who have nephritic syndrome will have a diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis.

362
00:25:56,100 --> 00:25:59,160
Patients who have nephrotic syndrome will have membranous nephropathy.

363
00:25:59,360 --> 00:26:00,780
That often shows up on exams.

364
00:26:01,360 --> 00:26:04,720
And then the final point is that if patients do survive late,

365
00:26:05,040 --> 00:26:09,640
then one of the common causes of death or increasingly common causes of death

366
00:26:09,640 --> 00:26:12,480
is accelerated coronary atherosclerosis.

367
00:26:12,640 --> 00:26:18,220
So these patients tend to have a coronary atherosclerosis that is accelerated for their age,

368
00:26:18,340 --> 00:26:19,800
which can also result in death,

369
00:26:19,880 --> 00:26:24,120
especially if they manage to avoid renal failure and infection as causes of death.

370
00:26:24,120 --> 00:26:28,740
The second disorder that we're going to discuss is called Sjövren syndrome.

371
00:26:29,300 --> 00:26:34,460
This is characterized by autoimmune destruction of the lacrimal glands and the salivary glands.

372
00:26:34,940 --> 00:26:38,200
The autoimmune destruction is mediated by lymphocytes,

373
00:26:38,620 --> 00:26:42,920
and so it is an example of a type 4 hypersensitivity reaction.

374
00:26:43,820 --> 00:26:47,060
Patients who have Sjövren syndrome will classically present with dry eyes

375
00:26:47,060 --> 00:26:49,100
because of the destruction of the lacrimal glands.

376
00:26:49,100 --> 00:26:53,540
They will present with dry mouth because of the destruction of the salivary glands.

377
00:26:54,080 --> 00:26:59,440
And in addition, patients can also complain or have the feature of recurrent dental caries.

378
00:26:59,940 --> 00:27:03,720
This is because, recall, that the salivary gland is normally producing fluid,

379
00:27:03,880 --> 00:27:06,040
and that fluid will wash over the surface of the teeth,

380
00:27:06,100 --> 00:27:07,840
and it will maintain some degree of cleanliness.

381
00:27:08,320 --> 00:27:10,180
However, if we lose the salivary glands,

382
00:27:10,280 --> 00:27:12,780
then that would increase the risk of development of dental caries.

383
00:27:13,500 --> 00:27:17,340
Women usually present at an older age, so it's classically an older woman,

384
00:27:17,720 --> 00:27:20,800
and they might complain of not being able to chew a cracker,

385
00:27:21,140 --> 00:27:24,720
and that's, again, because you need the secretions of the salivary gland

386
00:27:24,720 --> 00:27:26,300
to add the moisture to the cracker.

387
00:27:26,640 --> 00:27:29,460
And they also might complain of a feeling of dirt in their eyes,

388
00:27:29,500 --> 00:27:33,800
and that's because the lacrimal glands are constantly pushing fluid over the surface of the eye,

389
00:27:33,800 --> 00:27:38,480
which helps to wash away any dirt that might come into the eye from the outside environment.

390
00:27:38,900 --> 00:27:41,280
So these patients obviously don't have their lacrimal glands,

391
00:27:41,280 --> 00:27:44,340
and so they might get the experience of feeling dirt in their eyes.

392
00:27:45,040 --> 00:27:48,480
Now, although the classic presentation is that of dry eyes and dry mouth,

393
00:27:48,920 --> 00:27:53,700
it's important to recognize that patients can also have extra glandular manifestations.

394
00:27:54,120 --> 00:27:58,960
So, for example, patients can have joint problems, CNS problems, skin problems, etc.

395
00:27:59,520 --> 00:28:02,860
When patients have Sjövren's syndrome, it could be primary,

396
00:28:03,220 --> 00:28:05,980
or it can be associated with another autoimmune disorder.

397
00:28:06,140 --> 00:28:10,300
It's very common for Sjövren's to be associated with another autoimmune disorder.

398
00:28:10,300 --> 00:28:14,540
However, there's a whole variety of autoimmune disorders that can be associated with Sjövren's.

399
00:28:14,800 --> 00:28:17,600
However, the most common is rheumatoid arthritis,

400
00:28:17,820 --> 00:28:20,580
and so that's an important high-yield point to be aware of.

401
00:28:21,220 --> 00:28:25,860
Patients who have Sjövren's syndrome will often have something in their blood called rheumatoid factor.

402
00:28:26,280 --> 00:28:28,920
This is usually seen in rheumatoid arthritis.

403
00:28:29,060 --> 00:28:31,380
However, it can also be seen in patients with Sjövren's syndrome,

404
00:28:31,740 --> 00:28:34,880
and it's one more of those associations to be aware of.

405
00:28:34,880 --> 00:28:40,160
Now, what's interesting about rheumatoid factor is that if the patient had rheumatoid arthritis,

406
00:28:40,320 --> 00:28:41,640
you would expect rheumatoid factor.

407
00:28:41,780 --> 00:28:46,900
However, even if the patient doesn't have rheumatoid arthritis, meaning they have primary Sjövren's syndrome,

408
00:28:47,120 --> 00:28:50,400
they often also have rheumatoid factor in their blood.

409
00:28:50,740 --> 00:28:54,960
And so this is something that some people have recognized as a diagnostic criteria,

410
00:28:55,120 --> 00:28:57,780
i.e. the presence of rheumatoid factor in the blood.

411
00:28:57,780 --> 00:29:04,040
Patients who have Sjövren's syndrome will have an ANA, or an antinuclear antibody,

412
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:08,480
and in particular, they will have antibodies against ribonucleoproteins.

413
00:29:09,480 --> 00:29:13,540
Ribonucleoproteins are structures that are comprised of RNA and proteins.

414
00:29:13,740 --> 00:29:17,820
So, for example, the ribosomes are an example of ribonucleoproteins,

415
00:29:18,120 --> 00:29:21,960
and patients will often have antiribonucleoprotein antibodies.

416
00:29:21,960 --> 00:29:26,960
In particular, the two that they classically have is anti-SSA and anti-SSB.

417
00:29:27,780 --> 00:29:29,680
Now, there's no rocket science here.

418
00:29:29,920 --> 00:29:32,160
SS indicates that this is Sjövren's syndrome,

419
00:29:32,420 --> 00:29:36,260
and the A represents the A antibody, and the B represents the B antibody.

420
00:29:36,660 --> 00:29:40,740
So no one's ever going to ask you whether SSA and SSB are associated with Sjövren's

421
00:29:40,740 --> 00:29:42,140
because the name kind of gives it away.

422
00:29:43,100 --> 00:29:46,260
However, what you should be aware of regarding SSA and SSB

423
00:29:46,260 --> 00:29:50,140
is that, first of all, the epitopes are ribonucleoproteins.

424
00:29:50,140 --> 00:29:55,160
So SSA and SSB are antiribonucleoprotein antibodies, number one.

425
00:29:55,160 --> 00:30:01,540
Number two, patients who have SSA and SSB will often have extra-glandular manifestations.

426
00:30:01,540 --> 00:30:06,340
And number three, and perhaps the most high yield, is that patients who have SSA and SSB,

427
00:30:06,340 --> 00:30:13,160
the SSA in particular can cross the placenta if the patient's pregnant and can attack the baby or the fetus.

428
00:30:13,560 --> 00:30:16,320
And so these babies can develop neonatal lupus.

429
00:30:16,800 --> 00:30:21,540
Neonatal lupus is characterized by rash and hepatitis and thrombocytopenia and anemia.

430
00:30:21,540 --> 00:30:26,580
But most important, one of the key complications of neonatal lupus is congenital heart block.

431
00:30:26,940 --> 00:30:29,080
And so this is a high yield point that you want to take home.

432
00:30:29,500 --> 00:30:36,640
The association of pregnancy and SSA with neonatal lupus and in particular congenital heart block.

433
00:30:37,240 --> 00:30:43,000
Now interestingly, you can also see SSA and SSB in a subset of patients with lupus.

434
00:30:43,100 --> 00:30:48,500
So there is a fairly significant subset of patients with lupus that can also have SSA and SSB.

435
00:30:48,500 --> 00:30:53,780
And this is important because if they have SSA or SSB, they would again have the risk of neonatal lupus

436
00:30:53,780 --> 00:30:56,480
and would again have the risk of congenital heart block.

437
00:30:56,720 --> 00:31:02,360
So it's important when a patient has SLE and gets pregnant that we screen pregnant patients

438
00:31:02,360 --> 00:31:06,520
to make sure that they don't have anti-SSA or anti-SSB.

439
00:31:07,260 --> 00:31:11,760
And if they do, that we then watch for or recognize the risk for neonatal lupus

440
00:31:11,760 --> 00:31:14,800
with the feared complication of congenital heart block.

441
00:31:14,800 --> 00:31:18,960
There are some additional diagnostic criteria for Sjogren's syndrome.

442
00:31:19,540 --> 00:31:24,320
Recognize that dry eyes and dry mouth can occur from other things other than Sjogren's syndrome.

443
00:31:24,540 --> 00:31:28,800
In addition, SSA and SSB can be present due to other things other than Sjogren's syndrome.

444
00:31:29,160 --> 00:31:34,480
So one of the important diagnostic criteria is that we can do a biopsy of the minor salivary glands.

445
00:31:34,600 --> 00:31:39,320
What happens is we do a biopsy of the lip and we look at the minor salivary glands that are associated with the lip.

446
00:31:39,320 --> 00:31:43,880
And we can look for the presence of lymphocytes that are attacking the minor salivary glands.

447
00:31:43,960 --> 00:31:46,020
And that's called lymphocytic xylabinitis.

448
00:31:46,120 --> 00:31:51,040
And that's an important diagnostic criteria to establish a diagnosis of Sjogren's syndrome as well.

449
00:31:51,900 --> 00:31:55,400
To highlight this important diagnostic criteria, I've included this image.

450
00:31:55,780 --> 00:31:58,400
So on the left here is relatively normal tissue.

451
00:31:58,800 --> 00:32:03,060
This is a lip biopsy from a patient with suspected Sjogren's syndrome.

452
00:32:03,320 --> 00:32:06,600
And we, of course, have biopsied the minor salivary gland.

453
00:32:06,600 --> 00:32:12,720
Now, when you look at this, you'll notice that you see a bunch of glandular-like structures.

454
00:32:13,220 --> 00:32:16,940
Notice that some of the cells contain more pink cytoplasm.

455
00:32:17,040 --> 00:32:22,500
And these represent the serous cells, which are producing a more watery fluid.

456
00:32:23,020 --> 00:32:26,580
And other cells have a more abundant, clear cytoplasm.

457
00:32:27,020 --> 00:32:30,780
And these cells represent the mucinous cells, which produce a mucus-like fluid.

458
00:32:31,220 --> 00:32:35,360
The watery fluid and the mucus-like fluid will combine together to make saliva.

459
00:32:35,360 --> 00:32:38,160
On the left, again, this is relatively normal.

460
00:32:38,620 --> 00:32:43,380
When you move away from the left and you come to the center, you notice that there is this lymphocytic infiltrate,

461
00:32:43,880 --> 00:32:47,120
which is destroying some of the salivary gland.

462
00:32:47,620 --> 00:32:51,100
And so this would help us to make a diagnosis of Sjogren's syndrome.

463
00:32:51,920 --> 00:32:57,040
The reason we do the biopsy is to exclude other causes of destruction of the salivary gland.

464
00:32:57,780 --> 00:33:01,360
And so some of the things that we would want to exclude include, for example, amyloidosis.

465
00:33:01,360 --> 00:33:05,640
So we would want to confirm that there's no amyloid deposition within the gland.

466
00:33:06,280 --> 00:33:10,740
Similarly, we might also want to exclude a disease or a disorder called sarcoidosis,

467
00:33:10,880 --> 00:33:18,180
which is essentially a disorder in which you get non-caseating granulomas in multiple organs throughout the body.

468
00:33:18,180 --> 00:33:23,840
So it's important to remember that dry eyes and dry mouth can be due to other than Sjogren's syndrome,

469
00:33:24,280 --> 00:33:27,940
and we need to exclude other causes of dry eyes and dry mouth.

470
00:33:28,060 --> 00:33:30,440
And so a biopsy is particularly helpful.

471
00:33:31,060 --> 00:33:33,240
So in the end, if you want to summarize Sjogren's syndrome,

472
00:33:33,360 --> 00:33:38,460
there's essentially three major criteria that you need to look for in order to make a diagnosis.

473
00:33:39,020 --> 00:33:41,520
The first is going to be the presence of dry eyes.

474
00:33:41,520 --> 00:33:48,180
And when you think about dry eyes, we could test that clinically by looking for a decrease in tear production,

475
00:33:48,300 --> 00:33:50,720
and there are some clinical tests that we can use to do that.

476
00:33:51,120 --> 00:33:56,040
However, in the end, for your purposes, you should simply remember dry eyes criteria number one.

477
00:33:57,000 --> 00:34:04,320
Criteria number two is an ANA plus SSA or SSB or rheumatoid factor.

478
00:34:04,940 --> 00:34:09,160
And criteria number three is the presence of lymphocytic xyladenitis.

479
00:34:09,160 --> 00:34:15,340
Now, in the end, you only really need two of the three diagnostic criteria to make a diagnosis.

480
00:34:15,860 --> 00:34:21,620
However, I really wanted to emphasize the lymphocytic xyladenitis because it's a common board question

481
00:34:21,620 --> 00:34:26,400
where patients will come in with dry eyes and dry mouth, but they won't have the other diagnostic criteria,

482
00:34:26,600 --> 00:34:28,940
and you'll need to think about other disorders that could do that.

483
00:34:29,020 --> 00:34:35,180
For example, amyloidosis or sarcoidosis or drugs or prior radiation, etc.

484
00:34:35,180 --> 00:34:40,160
And so remember, the three important diagnostic criteria and the fact that we need two out of those three

485
00:34:40,160 --> 00:34:42,320
in order to make a diagnosis of Sjogren's syndrome.

486
00:34:43,340 --> 00:34:47,480
One final point about Sjogren's syndrome is that there's an increased risk for B-cell lymphoma.

487
00:34:47,900 --> 00:34:51,520
Now, patients with Sjogren's syndrome will normally have inflammation of the parotid glands,

488
00:34:51,880 --> 00:34:55,260
and so the parotid glands will actually be enlarged, and so when you look at these patients,

489
00:34:55,440 --> 00:34:57,420
both parotid glands are usually enlarged.

490
00:34:57,420 --> 00:35:01,680
Now, let's say that there's a patient that has Sjogren's syndrome, and they've had it for 10 or 15 years,

491
00:35:01,820 --> 00:35:06,100
and all of a sudden they show up where one of the parotid glands is becoming bigger than the other.

492
00:35:06,520 --> 00:35:11,520
Well, when one becomes bigger than the other, then you'd have to think about what's causing that enlargement on one side.

493
00:35:11,940 --> 00:35:15,560
And one of the things that can cause such an enlargement is B-cell lymphoma,

494
00:35:15,860 --> 00:35:21,120
and so this is an important association to be aware of, especially late in the disease course.

495
00:35:21,120 --> 00:35:25,920
The third disorder that we're going to talk about is systemic sclerosis.

496
00:35:26,340 --> 00:35:31,520
This is an autoimmune disorder in which you get sclerosis of the skin and visceral organs.

497
00:35:32,080 --> 00:35:36,560
Sclerosis simply means hardening of tissue, and in this particular disorder,

498
00:35:36,700 --> 00:35:40,860
the reason that you get hardening of tissue is because there's deposition of collagen within tissues.

499
00:35:41,460 --> 00:35:44,320
Of course, fibroblasts are the cells that deposit collagen,

500
00:35:44,320 --> 00:35:48,380
and so the underlying problem in this disorder is that the fibroblasts get activated,

501
00:35:48,380 --> 00:35:51,940
and they deposit collagen within multiple tissues.

502
00:35:52,600 --> 00:35:57,640
Concerning the pathogenesis, one theory is that in this particular disorder,

503
00:35:58,180 --> 00:36:05,000
you have an immune reaction against mesenchymal antigen, some unknown mesenchymal antigen,

504
00:36:05,360 --> 00:36:07,180
and so what I've drawn here is the blood vessel.

505
00:36:07,620 --> 00:36:11,340
These are endothelial cells, and underneath here is the connective tissue.

506
00:36:11,340 --> 00:36:16,360
So imagine that we have an immune reaction against an antigen within the connective tissue,

507
00:36:16,360 --> 00:36:19,120
or it might even be the endothelial cell itself.

508
00:36:19,600 --> 00:36:25,340
When we get this immune reaction, that results in a dysfunction of the endothelial cells.

509
00:36:25,860 --> 00:36:31,040
When we get dysfunction of the endothelial cells, there's increased expression of adhesion molecules.

510
00:36:31,400 --> 00:36:35,000
This then allows inflammatory cells to come into the connective tissue.

511
00:36:35,660 --> 00:36:42,540
Another problem that occurs is that there's going to be increased expression of a molecule called endothelion.

512
00:36:42,540 --> 00:36:48,800
Endothelion. Endothelion is an extremely strong vasoconstrictor, and that would result in constriction of the vessel.

513
00:36:49,180 --> 00:36:53,540
In addition, there would be decrease in the production of nitric oxide, and nitric oxide is a vasodilator.

514
00:36:54,260 --> 00:36:56,780
So in balance, we're going to have vasoconstriction.

515
00:36:56,780 --> 00:37:06,220
And then finally, the third thing that will happen is that the endothelial cells will begin to produce platelet-derived growth factor and TGF-beta.

516
00:37:06,920 --> 00:37:13,780
Now, TGF-beta and platelet-derived growth factor both have the ability to activate fibroblasts and to cause fibrosis.

517
00:37:14,780 --> 00:37:18,320
And so this will then result in fibrosis around the blood vessel.

518
00:37:18,320 --> 00:37:25,880
And over time, especially with the vasoconstriction, you'll get ischemia, and then that ischemia will result in additional fibrosis.

519
00:37:26,260 --> 00:37:32,720
And so as we get continued fibrosis, we eventually get end-organ damage, which then results in systemic sclerosis.

520
00:37:33,220 --> 00:37:35,820
Systemic sclerosis can be divided into two types.

521
00:37:35,940 --> 00:37:37,940
There is a limited type and a diffuse type.

522
00:37:38,380 --> 00:37:42,820
In the limited type, there is limited skin involvement with late visceral involvement.

523
00:37:43,220 --> 00:37:46,940
The prototype of this subtype is called the Crest syndrome.

524
00:37:46,940 --> 00:37:49,020
C stands for calcinosis.

525
00:37:49,160 --> 00:37:54,000
Calcinosis means that the patients get deposition of calcium in subcutaneous connective tissues.

526
00:37:54,440 --> 00:37:58,420
And the C also helps me to remember that the patients have an anti-centromere antibody.

527
00:37:59,080 --> 00:38:02,980
The R stands for Raynaud phenomena, so these patients will often have Raynaud phenomena.

528
00:38:03,460 --> 00:38:08,920
Again, remember that Raynaud phenomena, it usually occurs in the fingertips or the tip of the nose or the ear lobes, for example.

529
00:38:09,360 --> 00:38:12,620
And it results when the patient's exposed to cold or stressed.

530
00:38:12,620 --> 00:38:20,640
This then causes an arterial vasospasm, which then causes the tip of the finger, for example, to turn white and then eventually blue.

531
00:38:20,820 --> 00:38:23,820
And then when the vasospasm releases, to become red again.

532
00:38:24,220 --> 00:38:29,620
This is one of the earliest signs that it's seen in the limited type of systemic sclerosis.

533
00:38:30,740 --> 00:38:34,620
It's often seen years before any other symptoms or signs develop.

534
00:38:34,620 --> 00:38:39,180
But remember that Raynaud phenomena can also be seen in lupus patients.

535
00:38:39,320 --> 00:38:42,140
It can also be seen in normal patients.

536
00:38:42,460 --> 00:38:44,200
And so it's not specific for the disorder.

537
00:38:44,880 --> 00:38:47,620
The E in the Crest stands for esophageal dysmotility.

538
00:38:48,940 --> 00:38:51,020
Let's go back to the whiteboard to remind ourselves.

539
00:38:51,140 --> 00:38:51,620
Here is the esophagus.

540
00:38:52,380 --> 00:38:54,840
And then we have, of course, the esophagus feeding into the stomach.

541
00:38:54,840 --> 00:39:02,580
And so what can happen in these patients is that they can get fibrosis of the wall of the lower portion of the esophagus.

542
00:39:02,800 --> 00:39:04,600
That would result in dysmotility.

543
00:39:04,760 --> 00:39:08,960
And perhaps the patients could even have difficulty swallowing both solids and liquids.

544
00:39:09,280 --> 00:39:15,680
The reason for both solids and liquids is because we require peristalsis to bring down both solids and liquids.

545
00:39:15,780 --> 00:39:20,880
And if this wall was fibrosed, we would not have the normal peristalsis of the esophageal tube.

546
00:39:20,880 --> 00:39:30,560
In addition, because we get fibrosis in the lower portion of the esophagus, there can be disruption of the gastroesophageal junction.

547
00:39:31,340 --> 00:39:37,620
And if there's disruption of this junction, there can be reflux of acid from the stomach back into the esophagus.

548
00:39:38,040 --> 00:39:42,860
And so these patients can also have gastroesophageal reflux disease.

549
00:39:43,640 --> 00:39:47,000
The S stands for sclerodactyly.

550
00:39:47,000 --> 00:39:51,180
Sclerodactyly refers to fibrosis of the skin of the hands.

551
00:39:51,400 --> 00:39:56,320
And in fact, that's what we mean by limited skin involvement, that it usually involves the skin of the hand.

552
00:39:56,700 --> 00:39:59,920
Now, the fibrosis of the skin of the hand will make the skin tight.

553
00:40:00,320 --> 00:40:06,560
And so you'll lose the wrinkles on the skin that is present on the surface of the fingers, for example.

554
00:40:06,560 --> 00:40:15,800
And eventually, the tightness of the skin can even constrict the blood vessels so that the patients can get necrosis and ulceration of the fingertips.

555
00:40:16,760 --> 00:40:20,220
And then finally, the T represents telangiectasias of the skin.

556
00:40:21,020 --> 00:40:25,660
Telangiectasias are dilated capillaries, and this would result in red marks on the skin.

557
00:40:25,660 --> 00:40:34,220
Now, although the limited type can be summarized by crest, some patients can develop more extensive internal organ involvement.

558
00:40:34,600 --> 00:40:36,660
But overall, the prognosis is good.

559
00:40:37,840 --> 00:40:40,940
A second type of systemic sclerosis is the diffuse type.

560
00:40:41,340 --> 00:40:45,980
And in the diffuse type, you get diffuse involvement of skin with early visceral involvement.

561
00:40:46,420 --> 00:40:47,800
Now, any organ can be involved.

562
00:40:47,940 --> 00:40:50,880
Classic organs include vessels, giving you renal phenomena.

563
00:40:50,880 --> 00:40:55,100
The most commonly involved organ system is the GI tract.

564
00:40:55,520 --> 00:40:59,120
This can, for example, involve the stomach, leading to delayed emptying.

565
00:40:59,300 --> 00:41:02,020
It can involve the small bowel, leading to malabsorption.

566
00:41:02,500 --> 00:41:04,380
The esophagus is the classic site.

567
00:41:04,480 --> 00:41:07,340
And when you get esophageal involvement, you get dysphagia.

568
00:41:07,900 --> 00:41:14,100
You can also get another finding of gastroesophageal reflux disease, which you'll learn more about when you do your GI lectures.

569
00:41:14,700 --> 00:41:17,120
The lung is also an important site of involvement.

570
00:41:17,780 --> 00:41:20,020
Pulmonary involvement is the most common cause of death.

571
00:41:20,020 --> 00:41:27,080
So you should know that the patients with this disorder, when it involves a lung, they can get interstitial fibrosis, or they can develop pulmonary hypertension.

572
00:41:27,760 --> 00:41:30,780
The kidney is also an important organ that can be involved.

573
00:41:30,920 --> 00:41:33,180
The kidney is the second most common cause of death.

574
00:41:33,640 --> 00:41:37,660
The most feared complication of the kidney is called scleroderma renal crisis.

575
00:41:38,120 --> 00:41:42,500
And this is when patients get sudden onset of acute renal failure and severe hypertension.

576
00:41:42,500 --> 00:41:50,160
The good thing about this is that if you treat it with ACE inhibitors and you normalize the high blood pressure, that can lead to a resolution of the crisis.

577
00:41:50,760 --> 00:41:52,340
And so that's an important thing to be aware of.

578
00:41:52,760 --> 00:41:55,920
And then finally, patients can also get involvement of the heart.

579
00:41:55,920 --> 00:42:04,460
And so they can, for example, get heart failure because of the pulmonary hypertension, or they may actually get fibrosis of the cardiac tissue itself.

580
00:42:04,460 --> 00:42:12,520
So one important association to be aware of is that patients with the diffuse type will have antibodies against DNA topoisomerase 1.

581
00:42:12,740 --> 00:42:15,520
So limited type, anti-centromary antibodies.

582
00:42:15,940 --> 00:42:19,340
Diffuse type, DNA topoisomerase 1 antibodies.

583
00:42:20,620 --> 00:42:24,400
Here's a picture of a hand of a patient with systemic sclerosis.

584
00:42:24,400 --> 00:42:30,520
Notice here, and here, and here, that there are decreased wrinkles on the surface of the fingers.

585
00:42:30,660 --> 00:42:33,480
In fact, if you look at your own fingers, you'll be able to recognize wrinkles.

586
00:42:33,860 --> 00:42:35,780
But notice that there's a loss of wrinkles here.

587
00:42:35,840 --> 00:42:43,240
And that's because there's a tightening of the skin because of the deposition of fibrosis within the skin, making the skin thick and hard.

588
00:42:43,640 --> 00:42:45,680
And so you're not able to perceive these wrinkles.

589
00:42:46,160 --> 00:42:49,300
Another important thing to recognize is that there's this lesion here on the thumb.

590
00:42:49,620 --> 00:42:51,460
This so happens to be calcinosis.

591
00:42:51,460 --> 00:42:57,180
There's calcium that has deposited in the connective tissue underneath the skin there.

592
00:42:57,620 --> 00:42:58,920
And so this is an example.

593
00:42:59,080 --> 00:43:03,660
In fact, this patient had the limited type of systemic sclerosis.

594
00:43:04,240 --> 00:43:10,140
One important thing that I should mention here, the hand is commonly involved in both the limited and the diffuse type.

595
00:43:10,240 --> 00:43:13,660
However, in the limited type, it would be limited to the hand, usually.

596
00:43:14,140 --> 00:43:18,680
And in the diffuse type, it would involve the hand plus multiple other sites.

597
00:43:18,680 --> 00:43:21,440
For example, the limbs, the chest, the abdomen, etc.

598
00:43:21,720 --> 00:43:26,500
And so that would give you a clue that the patient actually may have diffuse scleroderma.

599
00:43:28,240 --> 00:43:32,120
The final disorder that we're going to talk about is called mixed connective tissue disease.

600
00:43:32,460 --> 00:43:38,680
This is an autoimmune disorder in which you get mixed features of lupus, systemic sclerosis, and polymyositis.

601
00:43:39,520 --> 00:43:42,520
Polymyositis is a disorder that we're going to talk about later in this course.

602
00:43:42,520 --> 00:43:46,980
So, for example, the patients can have arthritis, like you might see in a lupus patient.

603
00:43:47,400 --> 00:43:55,020
They can have esophageal dysmotility with some gastroesophageal reflux disease, as you might see in a patient with systemic sclerosis.

604
00:43:55,420 --> 00:44:01,560
And they can have myalgia with inflammation of the muscles or myositis, which you might see in a patient with polymyositis.

605
00:44:01,560 --> 00:44:06,840
Now, what's interesting about this disorder is that the patients usually begin very non-specifically.

606
00:44:07,000 --> 00:44:10,840
For example, they'll have fatigue, myalgia, low-grade fever, Raynaud.

607
00:44:11,120 --> 00:44:13,980
These are the characteristic initial signs.

608
00:44:14,440 --> 00:44:17,860
And so they won't really be placed into any particular connective tissue disorder.

609
00:44:18,020 --> 00:44:27,100
Over time, they begin to develop overlapping features, for example, of lupus and systemic sclerosis and polymyositis, as I've already described.

610
00:44:27,100 --> 00:44:31,220
And then that allows you to classify them as mixed connective tissue disease.

611
00:44:31,420 --> 00:44:33,340
There are some hallmarks of the disorder.

612
00:44:33,460 --> 00:44:38,260
For example, patients with MCTD or mixed connective tissue disease often have Raynaud's.

613
00:44:38,500 --> 00:44:40,740
They usually lack severe renal involvement.

614
00:44:41,140 --> 00:44:43,220
They usually lack severe CNS involvement.

615
00:44:43,740 --> 00:44:46,960
They usually have very severe arthritis and severe pulmonary hypertension.

616
00:44:47,260 --> 00:44:50,520
In fact, pulmonary hypertension is the major cause of death.

617
00:44:50,520 --> 00:44:57,420
But the most important take-home and the highest yield is that patients with mixed connective tissue disease will have an ANA,

618
00:44:57,820 --> 00:45:02,660
but they'll also have antibodies against U1 ribonucleoprotein.

619
00:45:02,940 --> 00:45:05,420
And that is a very important take-home point.

620
00:45:05,840 --> 00:45:09,240
So that concludes this section on more specific autoimmune disorders.

